      "reply_text": "A bridge carries changing loads through a network of beams, joints, supports, and foundations. Inspectors examine those parts because water, temperature changes, traffic, and ordinary wear can gradually alter how they work. A small crack may be harmless at first, yet repeated loading can make it grow. Rust can reduce the thickness of a steel member, while trapped water can damage concrete and the reinforcing bars inside it. Bearings must allow controlled movement as the structure expands and contracts. Drainage channels need to remain clear so that rain does not collect around vulnerable connections. During an inspection, engineers compare current observations with earlier records and photographs. They distinguish superficial marks from signs that require closer measurement. Some areas can be reached from the road, while others need platforms, ropes, or temporary access below the deck. The resulting report records what was seen, where it was found, and how urgently it should be addressed. Repairs can then be planned before a minor defect becomes a",
