      "reply_text": "Making a good cup of coffee begins with choosing beans that suit the drinker and the brewing method. Freshness matters, but so do storage conditions and the amount of time since roasting. Whole beans keep their aroma longer when they are protected from air, heat, and moisture. Grinding shortly before brewing exposes the surface needed for extraction without leaving the grounds open to the air for too long. A finer grind offers more contact with water, while a coarser grind usually works better for methods with longer contact times. Measuring the coffee and water makes it easier to repeat a result that tastes balanced. Water should be clean and hot enough to extract flavor without being left boiling through the entire process. During brewing, the water needs to reach the grounds evenly rather than finding a narrow path through one area. Afterward, tasting is more useful than following a rule without adjustment. A bitter result may suggest too much extraction, while a weak or sharp cup may call for changes",
